# Rank 1511 / CVE-2026-42217: OpenEXR's IDManifest variable-length integer
# reader shifted by an unbounded amount (CWE-758 use of undefined behavior
# feeding out-of-bounds parsing). readVariableLengthInteger in
# src/lib/OpenEXR/ImfIDManifest.cpp loops
#   do { ...bounds-check readPtr against endPtr...
#        value |= (uint64_t (byte & 127)) << shift;
#        shift += 7;
#   } while (byte & 128);
# so a manifest chunk with ten or more continuation bytes drives shift to
# 64 and beyond: shifting a uint64_t by its width is undefined behavior,
# the corrupted value then feeds string-list length decoding and indexes
# past the buffer. The fixing commit (confirmed --
# "Harden IDManifest parsing against illegal shift and string prefix OOB
# (#2378)"): a `if (shift >= 64) throw InputExc` before the shift; the
# common-prefix out-of-bounds half named in the message moves to PR
# #2377 and is not in this diff. .
#
# ATTENTION, no feature observes a shift amount. The bounds discipline in
# the loop is otherwise real -- readPtr is guarded against endPtr -- so
# the missing piece is specifically the shift-amount bound, and no rule
# family asks about it: VYQL-NUM-003 (TaintedIntegerSizeArithmetic) is the
# numeric family's nearest rule and covers size arithmetic on tainted
# values, not shift widths. Measured on the real code: scanning
# ImfIDManifest.cpp at both revisions reports the same low-confidence
#NullableDeref noise (the loop's deref at parent line 118 / fix line 125
# among them) and the same RawMemoryCopySize rows far below; nothing
# differs between the revisions and nothing touches the shift. A binding
# cannot express "the shift amount of this << is not provably below the
# type width" because no context token carries the amount or its bound --
# entered in ENGINE-GAPS.md (Medium: deciding what counts as a bounded
# shift is a guard judgement, like the lower-bound row). No definition
# change shipped. Spec cve_rank1511 records the unbounded-shift loop and
# the guarded form, both asserting reject VYQL-NUM-003.
#
# ## Recheck measurements
#
# Re-probed at the current knowledge base tip, where the `<<` itself IS
# labelled: match on the pre-fix ImfIDManifest.cpp shows
# `__binop.shl ... code.IntegerSizeArithmeticSink @ :122` (and @ :125 at
# the fix) from the shipped shl sink binding, so the sink half of
# VYQL-NUM-003 is in place and has been for some time. The rule still
# cannot fire, for two independent reasons, both re-measured:
#
# 1. No source. The file carries zero source labels of any concept; trace
#    -from core.UserControlledData -to code.IntegerSizeArithmeticSink
#    reports `0 source(s): 0 reach a sink, 0 dead-end`. The manifest
#    bytes arrive through `Xdr::read<CharPtrIO>` (19 `Xdr.?` unresolved
#    template call sites in this one file) and virtual Istream reads, so
#    taint needs the C/C++ out-parameter-buffer gap that is already on
#    file (read-style calls taint only the return value; the filled
#    buffer never carries it).
# 2. No discrimination even with a source. The rule's
#    `unless endpoint coveredBy core.IntegerOverflowGuard` is satisfied
#    only by a `*_MAX`/`numeric_limits` token in the function's binary
#    context; the fixing commit's guard is the literal comparison
#    `shift >= 64`, which produces `binary_shape:ID>=NUM` and no MAX
#    token, so the rule would fire on BOTH revisions.
#
# Three `--cache off` runs per revision over the whole checkout at
# 21eaa33bc^ and 21eaa33bc: 2112 findings each run at both revisions,
# byte-identical fingerprint sets; on ImfIDManifest.cpp exactly 12
# findings at each revision (2x VYQL-MEM-016 RawMemoryCopySize, 1x
# VYQL-MEM-007, 1x VYQL-MEM-012, 7x VYQL-MEM-006 NullableDeref, the
# loop's own deref at :118/:125 among them), differing only by the fix's
# 7-line offset. The tree's single VYQL-NUM-003 is
# src/wrappers/python/Imath.py:42 with the same fingerprint at both
# revisions; no numeric row lands on ImfIDManifest.cpp.
#
# The taint-free presence route was measured and is unshippable on
# precision. Probe bindings over `binary_shape`/`assign_op_shape` context
# tokens on the real tree: a shift by a variable (`<<ID` shape) appears
# in 652 functions; requiring no numeric `>=` bound in the function
# leaves 630; adding an accumulating `+=NUM`/`-=NUM` (the loop shape)
# leaves 55 across 25 files -- dominated by the OpenJPH block
# coders (ojph_block_decoder64.cpp x7, ojph_block_decoder32.cpp x6,
# encoders/variants x13), all ordinary entropy-coding code -- and the
# same probe still reports 54 functions at the FIXED revision, including
# IDManifest's own safe `serialize` writer (:573), because only
# readVariableLengthInteger gained an `ID>=NUM` comparison. One true
# positive in 55 with 54 hits surviving the fix is the measured-
# unshippable ratio, and tightening further means naming the shifted
# variable to correlate it with its bound, which is repro-shaped. The
# probe incidentally confirms `binary_shape` stays function-confined for
# C++ (the fix's in-function guard suppressed exactly that function's
# context hit while every other function kept firing).
#
# The 1st-pass note says the Medium ENGINE-GAPS entry ("deciding what
# counts as a bounded shift is a guard judgement, like the lower-bound
# row") was entered; no such entry is present in ENGINE-GAPS.md today.
# No definition is touched, so the OWASP Java and Python numbers are
# unaffected by this rank.

test "rank1511: continuation bytes shift past the type width"
  lang cpp
  file src/lib/OpenEXR/ImfIDManifest.cpp
  reject VYQL-NUM-003
  code
  ```
  #include <cstdint>
  #include <stdexcept>

  static uint64_t
  readVariableLengthInteger (const char*& readPtr, const char* endPtr)
  {
      uint64_t value = 0;
      int shift = 0;
      unsigned char byte = 0;
      do
      {
          if (readPtr >= endPtr)
          {
              throw std::runtime_error ("IDManifest too small for variable length integer");
          }
          byte = *(unsigned char*) readPtr++;
          value |= (uint64_t (byte & 127)) << shift;
          shift += 7;
      } while (byte & 128);
      return value;
  }
  ```

test "rank1511: the fixed revision rejects shifts at the type width"
  lang cpp
  file src/lib/OpenEXR/ImfIDManifest.cpp
  reject VYQL-NUM-003
  code
  ```
  #include <cstdint>
  #include <stdexcept>

  static uint64_t
  readVariableLengthInteger (const char*& readPtr, const char* endPtr)
  {
      uint64_t value = 0;
      int shift = 0;
      unsigned char byte = 0;
      do
      {
          if (readPtr >= endPtr)
          {
              throw std::runtime_error ("IDManifest too small for variable length integer");
          }
          if (shift >= 64)
          {
              throw std::runtime_error ("Invalid variable-length integer in IDManifest");
          }
          byte = *(unsigned char*) readPtr++;
          value |= (uint64_t (byte & 127)) << shift;
          shift += 7;
      } while (byte & 128);
      return value;
  }
  ```
